 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "SEA TERMINAL. Ferries to Liverpool. Don't be late: the captain isn't sentimental."
  },
  {
   "x": 46,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "THE TOWER OF REFUGE. Built 1832 on a rock in the bay so shipwrecked sailors had somewhere to wait. Still the poshest waiting room at sea. Founded by the chap who started the RNLI."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "DOUGLAS PROMENADE. Two miles of Victorian hotels. The Manx wind will find every one."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 49,
   "text": "DOUGLAS HEAD, south. Not on this map. Or any map, it's mostly cliff."
  },
  {
   "x": 45,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "HORSE TRAM. Running since 1876. Oldest surviving horse tramway in the world. Powered by one horse, several opinions."
  },
  {
   "x": 5,
   "y": 9,
   "text": "THE LAXEY WHEEL, 'Lady Isabella', 1854. Nearly 22 metres across. The largest working waterwheel on Earth. This is a very approximate model."
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "GAIETY THEATRE. Victorian, ornate, haunted by exactly the right amount of ghost."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 32,
   "text": "ISLE OF MAN STEAM RAILWAY. Narrow gauge, wide smile, puffs of coal smoke and nostalgia."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "MANX KIPPERS. Smoked herring. The smell announces itself three streets away."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 39,
   "text": "LOAGHTAN FIELD. Four horns, one opinion: leave us alone. They are mouse-coloured, and they know it."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 48,
   "text": "DOUGLAS HEAD. Cliffs this way. Seagulls that way. Chips somewhere between."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 17,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Sir William Hillary, who founded the RNLI in 1824, lived in Douglas. His Tower of Refuge stands on Conister Rock.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "douglas_hillary",
   "title": "Sir William Hillary"
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "douglas_name",
   "title": "Name: Douglas",
   "text": "Douglas, Doolish in Manx, is named after two rivers that meet here, the Dhoo and the Glass. In Manx Gaelic, as in Irish, dhoo means black and glass means green or grey. So the island's capital is named after the colour of its water, which is very honest of it."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 18,
   "plaque": "douglas_three_legs",
   "title": "The Three Legs of Man",
   "text": "The three armoured legs on the Manx flag, a triskelion, have stood for the island since the Middle Ages. The Latin motto is Quocunque Jeceris Stabit: whichever way you throw it, it will stand. Ellan Vannin, the Isle of Man, is nothing if not stubborn."
  },
  {
   "x": 42,
   "y": 23,
   "plaque": "douglas_manannan",
   "title": "Manannan's Cloak",
   "text": "In Manx legend the island belongs to Manannan mac Lir, the sea god, who throws a cloak of mist around it to hide it from raiders. Some say Mannin, the island's name, comes from him. Tradition says he was paid his rent in bundles of rushes on Midsummer Eve. A very reasonable landlord."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 34,
   "plaque": "douglas_basking_sharks",
   "title": "Basking Sharks",
   "text": "Most summers basking sharks cruise the Manx coast, feeding with their huge mouths wide open. They are the second largest fish on Earth, as long as a bus, and eat nothing but plankton. Harmless, unhurried and best admired from a boat that keeps its distance."
  },
  {
   "x": 37,
   "y": 34,
   "plaque": "man_palace_casino",
   "title": "The Palace, Douglas",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: The Palace. The Isle of Man isn't part of the United Kingdom: it's a Crown Dependency with its own parliament, Tynwald, which writes its own laws, gambling included. The island prints its own pound notes too, each worth exactly a pound sterling.",
   "dx": 0.475
  },
  {"x": 36, "y": 22, "plaque": "man_gaelg", "title": "Manx Gaelic", "local": {"gv": "Yn Ghaelg"}, "text": "Ned Maddrell, a fisherman from Glen Chass, died on 27 December 1974 and is remembered as the last native speaker of Manx. He had been recorded in 1948 by the Irish Folklore Commission. The language did not end with him: the Manx Language Society was founded in 1899, and in 2001 Bunscoill Ghaelgagh opened in St John's, a primary school that teaches in Manx. Moghrey mie means good morning."},
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 21,
   "plaque": "arts_te_brown",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "T. E. Brown",
   "text": "Thomas Edward Brown, born in Douglas in 1830, was a schoolmaster at Clifton in Bristol and the Isle of Man's national poet. His Fo'c's'le Yarns of 1881 told Manx stories in the island's own English, and his short poem 'My Garden' gave the language a line everybody knows and nobody can place: 'A garden is a lovesome thing, God wot!' He died in 1897. The gardens carry on regardless."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "arts_ellan_vannin",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Ellan Vannin",
   "text": "'Ellan Vannin' is the Isle of Man in Manx, and the song of that name, a poem by Eliza Craven Green from 1854 set to music, is the island's unofficial anthem, sung wherever Manx people gather away from home. In 1997 the Bee Gees recorded it: Barry, Robin and Maurice Gibb were born in Douglas before the family left for Manchester and then Australia. A statue of the three of them stands on the promenade, mid-stride, forever on their way to the ferry."
  }
 ],
